\subsection*{Part A-0. Preparation of a diluted starch solution}
 \begin{steps}
        \newstep[] Obtain about 50 mL of 0.2\% stock starch in a small beaker. Clean and rinse a 20mL volumetric pipette with the starch solution.
         \newstep[] Using a 20mL volumentric piper, pipet 20ml of the stock starch (0.2\%) into a 100mL flask.
        \newstep[]  Fill up the flask with distilled water until the 100mL mark. This is be the 0.04\% starch solution (the starch solution) you will use through out the experiment.
\end{steps}      
        


\subsection*{Part A-1. Starch serial dilution}
 \begin{steps}
        \newstep[] In the following you will prepare a series of starch dilutions with concentrations: 0.04\%, 0.02\%, 0.0125\%, $5.0\times 10^-5$\%, and $2.5\times 10^-5$\%.
        \newstep[] Find five jumbo test tubes with stoppers and label them as A, B, C, D, and E.
        \newstep[] Using a 10mL Mohr's pipette, pipet 10ml of distilled water into test tubes B, C, D, and E.

       \newstep[] Obtain about 25 mL of 0.04\% starch in a small beaker. Clean and rinse a 10mL Mohr's pipette with the enzyme solution.
       
         \import{\chapterlabel/}{goodlabpractice}
       
     \newstep[] Using the Mohr's pipet, pipet 10ml of starch into test tube A (test tube A is the starch 0.04\% solution) and 10ml into test tube B (test tube A is the starch 0.02\% solution) . 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube B into test tube C. Close test tube C with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube C is the starch 0.0125\% solution. 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube C into test tube D. Close test tube D with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube D is the starch $5.0\times 10^{-3}$\% solution. 
      \newstep[] Pipet 10ml of test tube D into test tube E. Close test tube E with a stopper, and mix well its content by inverting the tube 3 times at constant speed. Test tube E is the starch $2.5\times 10^{-3}$\% solution. Now pipet 10mL out of tube E and discart.
      
      \newstep[] Add no more than 1drop of the \ce{I_2}-\ce{I^-} solution into each test and mix all test tubes well until the color is homogeneous. With your serial dilution done properly, all test tubes should have 10mL of liquid and you should see test tube A darker in comparison to the other test tubes with intensity decreasing from test tube A to E.
      
\end{steps}
